            {
                "entry_id": "54ab3699-3803-306d-99c6-14ff893ac347",
                "word": "clerihew",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "534ea841-cdb1-428d-adba-979782564721",
                        "entry_id": "54ab3699-3803-306d-99c6-14ff893ac347",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "clerihews"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "e0af2574-6771-3c37-9842-f7183f8207b6",
                        "entry_id": "54ab3699-3803-306d-99c6-14ff893ac347",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A humorous rhyme of four lines with the rhyming scheme AABB, usually regarding a person mentioned in the first line."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "dca8e463-4d59-4707-a439-8d611ba5180c",
                                "sense_id": "e0af2574-6771-3c37-9842-f7183f8207b6",
                                "text": "CLERIHEW CONTEST. CNV announces a clerihew contest, with the best examples to be published in this newsletter."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "465c1e97-6635-40bf-a675-4cd5bfe7ce87",
                                "sense_id": "e0af2574-6771-3c37-9842-f7183f8207b6",
                                "text": "He [Peter Warlock] was the writer of brilliant limericks and clerihews and had a fine line in barbed invective."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "513bf091-9527-4dfe-9baa-b126890e5df3",
                                "sense_id": "e0af2574-6771-3c37-9842-f7183f8207b6",
                                "text": "A clerihew must contain the subject's name in the first line, be four lines in length, consist of two sets of rhyming couplets, have third and fourth lines longer than the first and second, and take a whimsical rather than cynical view of its subject."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "713b6138-7dc3-475a-856b-352b17368653",
                                "sense_id": "e0af2574-6771-3c37-9842-f7183f8207b6",
                                "text": "Among other clerihew writers one can find the name of J[ohn] R[onald] R[euel] Tolkien, the author of The Hobbit and the most famous at present trilogy The Lord of the Ring[s] widely read and enjoyed by adults and children alike.",
                                "translation": "Ministry of Education of the Russian Soviet Federative Socialist Republic",
                                "english": "Ministry of Education of the Russian Soviet Federative Socialist Republic"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "719b7daa-9e87-4aad-8207-c249fbf14754",
                                "sense_id": "e0af2574-6771-3c37-9842-f7183f8207b6",
                                "text": "This form was initiated by Edmund Clerihew Bentley who throughout his life kept churning out Clerihews, the name they ultimately became known by; he had published three collections under the name E. Clerihew."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "72783784-2c05-4039-b869-7b32afa83ff7",
                                "sense_id": "e0af2574-6771-3c37-9842-f7183f8207b6",
                                "text": "Warner Allen's own creation, the wine merchant William Clerihew, had appeared in 'Tokay of the Comet Year', a short story published in 1930, and also in the book Mr. Clerihew: Wine Merchant three years later. […] The Clerihew name was a hat-tip to [Edmund Clerihew] Bentley, who had, long before, devised the humorous four-line verse form known as the clerihew."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
